<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mathrafal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mathrafal is a site near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meifod">Meifod</a> in Montgomeryshire, mid-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, traditionally the chief court of the kings and princes of Powys from the 9th century. A ringwork and bailey castle survives there, probably built in the late 12th or early 13th century. The site was abandoned after it was destroyed in 1212, during the wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> and the princes of Wales.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mathrafal.jpg" alt="Mathrafal" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tipcake · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mathrafal_7_Mawrth_2026.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
